\Section{Conditions for revisiting the decision}
Revisit the growth plan if later cohorts' six-month cash recovery falls toward the earlier window, if a valid longer campaign comparison fails to cover media cost, or if young-cohort outcomes fall materially below forecast. Historical blended CAC does not establish the return from another advertising dollar.


\SectionDivider{01}{Studio North\newline and the customer experience}{The membership business, customer needs and possible sources of value and friction.}
\PageTitle{01 / BUSINESS OVERVIEW}{The membership experience\newline behind the economics}{Studio North serves independent professionals seeking faster creative work or stronger skills.}
Templates support immediate work; guided lessons build capabilities over repeated use. A monthly membership is valuable when those benefits recur often enough to justify the next payment. Optional workshops and add-ons expand spending but do not by themselves establish durable membership value.
\ReportFigure{business-journey}{Conceptual. A broad conceptual journey through the Studio North membership. Benefits and friction points are possible experiences, not measured sentiment or population conversion rates.}{business-journey.png}{4.3in}
\Section{Potential sources of renewal friction}
An unclear plan choice can delay subscription. A difficult first task can prevent a member from experiencing value. Later, a lack of time or an unmet learning goal may weaken the reason to renew. These are business hypotheses for investigation; the source records do not establish their causes.
\Section{Evidence available to the management team}
The CRM records stated needs and baseline characteristics. Product events show activity, transactions show spending, renewal records show continuation, and feedback captures selected perceptions. Together, these sources support a more informative diagnosis than a renewal rate alone. Tables~\ref{tab:ucv-people}--\ref{tab:ucv-experiment-assignments} classify all eight sources using the Universal Customer View. The appendix documents parameters, validation and reproducibility.
\Boundary{All records describe an illustrative business and are synthetic. Recorded variable costs and acquisition spending exclude fixed operating costs, financing and taxes; this analysis does not establish company profitability.}

\SectionDivider{02}{Observed history\newline and the forecast horizon}{How a renewal model supports a common comparison of mature and newly acquired cohorts.}
\PageTitle{02 / PURPOSE OF THE LTV MODEL}{Extrapolation makes young\newline cohorts comparable}{A common twelve-receipt horizon combines observed contribution with an explicit forecast of the unobserved remainder.}
\Section{What the model contributes}
Recent cohorts have not yet had the opportunity to demonstrate a full year of renewals. The retention model estimates how continuation varies across customers, acquisition channels, stated needs and joining months. It uses those patterns to forecast the remaining renewal path. A revenue model then translates retained membership into expected contribution, which is discounted and compared with acquisition cost.
\ReportFigure{retention-forecast}{Observed and forecast. Observed and forecast retention for cohorts with complete, intermediate and minimal renewal histories. Solid points are observed; dashed continuations are forecasts. Examples are selected by history length, not outcome.}{retention-forecast.pdf}{3.3in}
\Evidence{Saved model output: \Code{forecast_curve.csv}. Original cohort denominators are preserved. Shading shows the 5th--95th posterior parameter range; the vertical marker identifies the observation boundary.}
\Section{The information behind each forecast}
The mature cohort supplies a fully observed reference. The intermediate cohort combines its own renewal history with evidence from other cohorts. The newest cohort has only its acquisition month and therefore \textbf{zero observed renewal decisions}; its forecast depends primarily on comparable customers and model assumptions.
\Boundary{Extrapolation supports an earlier assessment of cohort value; it does not turn future outcomes into observed facts. These bands describe parameter uncertainty in expected paths, excluding customer-level process variation and changes in the business. The model specification, historical prediction checks and valuation assumptions appear later in the report.}

