\Section{Cohort trajectories at comparable ages}
The vertical position of differently colored curves at the \emph{same} age supports an aligned comparison. Later cohorts lying above earlier cohorts suggest improving retention; they need not dominate at every month. The chronological color key replaces an unwieldy 24-item legend. A curve with only its acquisition point has no observed renewal history.
\Section{Customer counts and revenue answer different questions}
Higher revenue retention can coexist with customer losses when remaining members upgrade or buy add-ons. A retention improvement should therefore be checked against contribution and acquisition recovery in Figure~\ref{fig:cash-triangle}, and against comparable customer groups in Figure~\ref{fig:channel-retention}.
\Takeaway{The trajectory matters as well as the cutoff. A stronger first month does not establish longer customer life, while a customer-count decline can coexist with more spending by the members who remain.}

